
Lambda & Recursion — Digital Notes
This topic completes Module III and builds directly on functions.

The two main concepts are:

Lambda functions → small, one-time functions.

Recursion → a function calling itself to solve a smaller version of the same problem.


1. Lambda Function
A lambda is a small, anonymous (unnamed) function written in a single line.

Normal function
def square(x):

    return x * x


Lambda equivalent
lambda x: x * x


The lambda has:

lambda parameter: expression


Example:

lambda x: x + 10


means:

Take x and return x + 10.


2. Why is it Called Anonymous?
A normal function has a name:

def over_limit(p):

    return p[1] > 25.05


The function's name is:

over_limit


A lambda usually has no name:

lambda p: p[1] > 25.05


It is created and used where it is needed.

Think of it as a disposable shim: make it for one small job and don't store it.


3. Lambda with sorted()
One of the most common uses of lambda is sorting.

Suppose we have:

parts = [

    ("PE-0137", 25.02),

    ("PE-0201", 24.90),

    ("PE-0345", 25.09)

]


Each item contains:

(part number, diameter)


To sort by diameter:

sorted(parts, key=lambda p: p[1])


Result:

PE-0201 → 24.90

PE-0137 → 25.02

PE-0345 → 25.09


Understanding key=lambda
key=lambda p: p[1]


means:

"When sorting, use the second element of each part."

Remember Python indexing:

p[0] → first element

p[1] → second element



4. Sorting in Reverse
To sort from largest to smallest:

sorted(parts, key=lambda p: p[1], reverse=True)


reverse=True reverses the sorting order.


5. Sorting by Part Number
We can change the lambda:

sorted(parts, key=lambda p: p[0])


Now Python sorts according to the part number.

This shows why lambda is useful: we can quickly tell Python which field to use.


6. Finding the Worst Part
We can also use lambda with max().

worst = max(

    parts,

    key=lambda p: abs(p[1] - 25.00)

)


Here:

p[1]


is the diameter.

And:

abs(p[1] - 25.00)


calculates how far the diameter is from the nominal value 25.00.

So max() finds the part furthest from 25.00.


7. When Should We Use Lambda?
Use lambda when the operation is:

Very small

Simple

Used only once

Easy to understand without a separate name

Example:

sorted(parts, key=lambda p: p[1])


This is clear and short.


8. When NOT to Use Lambda
If the logic is complicated or will be reused, create a normal function.

Instead of:

lambda p: complicated_calculation(p)


use:

def calculate_something(p):

    # complicated logic

    return result


Easy rule
If the lambda needs a comment to explain what it does, it probably deserves a name.


